# Reverse proxy for the services VM.
#
# Bound to the tailnet IP only — nothing here is reachable from the LAN or the
# internet. Certificates come from Let's Encrypt via DNS-01, because Let's
# Encrypt cannot reach a tailnet-only host for HTTP-01. (The VPS is public and
# uses HTTP-01 instead, so it needs no DNS plugin.)
#
# ONE wildcard certificate for *.svc.idanreed.com rather than a cert per
# hostname. Per-hostname certs publish every internal service name to public
# Certificate Transparency logs — a searchable index of what you run.
#
# DNS: *.svc.idanreed.com is a public Cloudflare A record pointing at this
# host's tailnet IP. The address is CGNAT space and only routable from inside
# the tailnet, so publishing it is not an exposure. This replaces the CoreDNS
# instance the old design called for and never deployed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Forward auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Import this into a handle block to put Authentik in front of a service that
# has no usable auth of its own:
#
#	handle @dozzle {
#		import protected
#		reverse_proxy localhost:10451
#	}
#
# Defined BEFORE the site block on purpose: Caddy resolves `import` while
# parsing, so a snippet defined below its first use fails with "File to
# import not found".
#
# What it relies on (all exist now):
#   1. A Proxy Provider (forward_single) + application per protected service,
#      assigned to Authentik's embedded outpost — defined in
#      headscale-vps/authentik/blueprints/custom/forward-auth.yaml. bentopdf
#      is the pilot entry.
#   2. AUTHENTIK_COOKIE_DOMAIN=idanreed.com on the Authentik containers, so the
#      session cookie is valid across auth.idanreed.com and *.svc.idanreed.com
#      (already set in headscale-vps/authentik/compose.yaml).
#   3. The VPS Caddy trusting X-Forwarded-* from tailnet sources
#      (trusted_proxies in headscale-vps/modules/caddy.nix) — see below.
#
# The hop rides the TAILNET (the VPS's MagicDNS node name), not the public
# route, and that is load-bearing: the embedded outpost routes forward-auth
# requests BY X-Forwarded-Host, and any Caddy replaces that header unless the
# source is a trusted proxy. Dialled publicly, the VPS's Caddy sees our home
# IP (untrusted, not pinnable), rewrites X-Forwarded-Host to auth.idanreed.com,
# and the outpost 404s every check. Over the tailnet the source is our own
# 100.64.0.0/10 address, which modules/caddy.nix trusts. tls_server_name +
# Host pin the request to the auth vhost, whose certificate is for
# auth.idanreed.com.
#
# STILL a lockout hazard per service: importing this into a handle whose
# external host has no matching provider entry in that blueprint leaves the
# outpost with nothing to match the Host against — every request 404s or
# redirect-loops instead of reaching the service. Add the blueprint entry and
# deploy the VPS first, then flip the import here.
#
# ⚠ THE CLIENT-SUPPLIED `X-Authentik-*` STRIP IS NOT IN THIS SNIPPET. It is a
# single site-level `request_header -X-Authentik-*` at the top of the site
# block below, and it has to be there — see the comment on it. Adding one here
# instead would be worse than useless: caddy's directiveOrder
# (caddyconfig/httpcaddyfile/directives.go, v2.11.2) puts `forward_auth` at
# entry 44 and `request_header` at entry 45, so a bare `request_header` beside
# this `forward_auth` runs AFTER it and would delete exactly the headers the
# outpost had just copied in — every protected app silently loses its
# identity. Wrapping both in a `route` fixes the order but re-indents this
# block, which is a documented breaking change for
# tests/suites/forward-auth.nix (it injects `tls_trust_pool` into the
# `transport http` block below by an indentation-anchored regex, and fails
# loudly when it stops matching). Site level needs neither.
(protected) {
	forward_auth https://headscale-vps.tailnet.idanreed.com {
		uri /outpost.goauthentik.io/auth/caddy
		copy_headers X-Authentik-Username X-Authentik-Groups X-Authentik-Email X-Authentik-Name X-Authentik-Uid
		header_up Host auth.idanreed.com
		transport http {
			tls_server_name auth.idanreed.com
		}
	}
}

*.svc.idanreed.com {
	bind {$TAILNET_IP}

	tls {
		dns cloudflare {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
	}

	# Nothing outside this proxy may ASSERT an Authentik identity. Applies to
	# every vhost below, protected or not, and to every route added later
	# without anyone remembering this line exists — which is the point.
	#
	# WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT IN `(protected)`: caddy's directiveOrder
	# (v2.11.2) is `... forward_auth (44), request_header (45), ... handle
	# (53) ...`. A `request_header` written beside the snippet's
	# `forward_auth` would run AFTER it and delete the outpost's own headers.
	# At SITE level it is still entry 45, but the things it has to beat are
	# the `handle` blocks at entry 53 — so it runs before every one of them,
	# and therefore before the `forward_auth` nested inside them. Verified
	# with `caddy adapt`: this handler is srv0's first route, ahead of the
	# subroute holding the vhosts.
	#
	# WHAT IT ACTUALLY BUYS, stated accurately — the obvious version of this
	# claim is wrong, and was checked before being written down. `caddy adapt`
	# shows `copy_headers` expanding, per header, into an UNCONDITIONAL
	# `request_header -X-Authentik-<Field>` plus a `set` guarded by a
	# non-empty matcher, all inside `handle_response` matching `status_code
	# 2xx`. So the FIVE NAMED headers are already stripped on every 2xx auth
	# response, including the paths a provider's skip_path_regex waves through
	# (Firefly's `^/api/.*`, which answers 200 carrying no headers at all).
	# "A client sets X-Authentik-Username and Firefly's remote_user_guard
	# believes it" does not survive contact with the adapted JSON.
	#
	# What is genuinely open, and what this closes:
	#   1. THE REST OF THE FAMILY. The outpost also emits X-authentik-jwt and
	#      the X-authentik-meta-* set. `copy_headers` names none of them, so a
	#      client-supplied one is forwarded verbatim by caddy's
	#      forward-unknown-headers default. Nothing consumes them today —
	#      which is precisely the state in which someone switches one on.
	#   2. ORDER INDEPENDENCE. Those five are protected only as a SIDE EFFECT
	#      of how copy_headers expands. Shorten that list, or land a caddy
	#      whose expansion differs, and the protection leaves silently. A
	#      prefix strip depends on none of it.
	#   3. UNPROTECTED ROUTES. kavita, audiobookshelf, ntfy and the rest never
	#      import (protected) at all, so nothing was stripping anything there.
	#
	# `*` is a real prefix wildcard and the case written here is irrelevant:
	# the runtime lowercases both pattern and field name
	# (modules/caddyhttp/headers/headers.go, the `HasSuffix(fieldName, "*")`
	# arm). Same move as the X-API-Key strip on the Syncthing route below.
	#
	# MEASURED, not reasoned (caddy 2.11.2, stub outpost + echo backend, a
	# request carrying `X-Authentik-Username: ATTACKER` and
	# `X-Authentik-Jwt: FORGED`):
	#   with this line,    outpost emits Username -> USER=outpost-user JWT=[]
	#   with this line,    outpost emits nothing  -> USER=[]            JWT=[]
	#   without it,        outpost emits Username -> USER=outpost-user JWT=FORGED
	#   without it,        outpost emits nothing  -> USER=[]            JWT=FORGED
	# i.e. the real identity still arrives (this does not break auth), the
	# five named headers were never the hole, and X-Authentik-Jwt was.
	request_header -X-Authentik-*

	@arcane host arcane.svc.idanreed.com
	handle @arcane {
		# Forward auth, and of everything in this file it is the entry
		# with the largest blast radius: Arcane mounts the Docker socket,
		# so its web UI is a ROOT-EQUIVALENT interface for this host. The
		# _overview.md row has said FwdAuth all along; this implements it.
		# Its own login stays underneath, so it fails closed either way.
		#
		# 🚨 ROLLOUT ORDER IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE. The blueprint entry plus a
		# VPS rebuild must land BEFORE this route syncs, because the thing
		# you would normally use to fix a broken deploy IS Arcane. If it
		# does lock out, the recovery paths are the loopback publish over
		# SSH (`curl localhost:10000`) and `nixos-rebuild switch
		# --target-host`, neither of which goes through Caddy — but plan
		# not to need them.
		#
		# Nothing calls Arcane's API through Caddy: the gitops suite drives
		# it from inside the VM over loopback, and the UI is browser-only.
		import protected
		reverse_proxy localhost:10000
	}

	@paperless host paperless.svc.idanreed.com
